# Wrangler-runtime secrets for `wrangler dev` (used by `npm run preview` and
# `npm run smoke`). Copy to .dev.vars (gitignored) and fill in real values.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ALONGSIDE mise.local.toml
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are different things and do not overlap:
#
#   mise.local.toml  →  sets shell env vars     →  Astro reads at BUILD time
#                                                  (PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY)
#
#   .dev.vars        →  wrangler reads at start →  Worker reads at RUNTIME
#                                                  via locals.runtime.env.*
#                                                  (BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY,
#                                                   TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY)
#
# Each variable lives in exactly one file. Worker-runtime secrets are kept
# out of shell env on purpose — only wrangler reads .dev.vars, so they
# can't leak into other tools' process.env.
#
# In PRODUCTION, .dev.vars is not used at all. Real values are set with:
#   wrangler secret put BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY
#   wrangler secret put TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY
#
# WHAT WORKS WITH THE DEFAULTS BELOW
# ───────────────────────────────────
# The defaults give you a working form for everything EXCEPT the actual
# Buttondown call: form renders → Turnstile renders + always passes (test
# secret) → /api/subscribe verifies token → Buttondown call returns 401
# (the placeholder API key isn't real) → endpoint returns 502 to client.
# That's enough to exercise the entire pipeline locally.
#
# To test the full happy path locally, replace BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY with a
# real key from a free Buttondown account (Settings → Programming → API).
#
# Turnstile test keys (always pass / never block) are documented at:
#   https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/troubleshooting/testing/

BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY=your-buttondown-api-key
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA

# PREVIEW_SIGNING_KEY signs the expiring links that reveal a scheduled post
# before its pubDate (see CLAUDE.md → "Scheduled publishing"). Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# and in production:
#   wrangler secret put PREVIEW_SIGNING_KEY
#
# NOTE — this one bends the rule described above. The other two secrets are
# read only by wrangler; this one is ALSO read from this file by
# scripts/preview-link.mjs running on the host, because HMAC requires the
# minting side and the verifying side to share a key. It is the only such
# case. It is a signing key for unpublished blog drafts, not a credential for
# anything external, so the blast radius of the exception is small.
#
# Optional: if unset, the worker rejects every preview link and only the
# *.workers.dev host unlock remains. Nothing else breaks — so this is left
# EMPTY on purpose.
#
# Unlike the two placeholders above (a Turnstile test secret that only pairs
# with the test site key, and a Buttondown key that 401s), a signing key
# placeholder would be fully FUNCTIONAL: whatever string ships here works, and
# everyone who copied this file would share one key that is public in git.
# Anyone could then mint a valid preview link for any slug. Generate your own:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
PREVIEW_SIGNING_KEY=
